# Project overview

Flask monolith finance tracker (loans + spending + daily budget). No separate frontend framework.

## Layout

- `@app.py` — routes, domain logic, JSON persistence (large single module)
- `templates/` — Jinja pages (`daily_budget.html`, `spending.html`, `loans.html`, …)
- `static/` — vanilla JS/CSS (`daily_budget.js`, `spending.js`, `script.js`, `style.css`)
- `tests/` — `unittest` modules (not pytest-first; run with `python -m unittest …`)
- `data.json` — local persistence (gitignored); production has its own copy + `.env`
- `.cursor/rules/` — agent context; keep learnings here for future agents

## Stack / runtime

- Python Flask app: `python app.py` or `./start.sh` (sources `.env` if present)
- Auth users from `FINANCE_TRACKER_USERS` (`user:pass,user2:pass2`); if unset, local defaults `admin`/`admin123` and `user`/`user123`
- Session login required for app routes; APIs return JSON
- Static assets may be cache-busted via helpers in `app.py` — hard-refresh if UI looks stale after deploy

## Data model (high level)

- Top-level JSON: `{ "users": { "<username>": { "spending": … } }, "loans": … }`
- Per-user spending lives under `users.<user>.spending` (`transactions`, `statements`, `monthly_insights`, `daily_budget`)
- Manual daily spends are normal outgoing transactions with `source: "manual"` and a `date` (`YYYY-MM-DD`)

## Tests

```sh
.venv/bin/python -m unittest tests.test_daily_budget tests.test_daily_budget_ui -v
```

Prefer extending existing unittest files; UI presence checks often assert substrings in rendered HTML + JS source.

## Production

Only deploy when the user explicitly asks. Follow `@.cursor/rules/production-deployment.mdc`.

## Keeping agent context fresh

When you discover durable repo knowledge (invariants, APIs, UX contracts, prod quirks), update the relevant `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` (or add a focused new rule) in the same PR/commit. See root `AGENTS.md`.
